    "https://ripon.edu/masters-programs/": {
      "title": "Master’s Programs",
      "description": "Master’s Programs |  Ripon College Skip to Main Content About About Ripon Why Ripon Ripon at a Glance Mission and Vision Strategic Plan Leadership Community Resources Ripon College History Ripon, WI Fact Sheet Support Ripon Support Giving Ways to Give Where to Give Impact of Giving Engage Engage Alumni Events Alumni Weekend R Connections Update Information Parents and Families Volunteer Opportunities Oral Histories Project (PCI) Community Resources Community Resources Willmore Center Ceresco Prairie Conservancy Art Galleries Give to Ripon Show your support for the college and its students with a donation. Give to Ripon Academics Overview Overview Areas of Study Master’s Programs Centers and Resources STEM Faculty Directory Endowed Chairs and Professors Course Catalog Academic Calendar Experiential Learning Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry Lane Library & Archives Lane Library Library Information Library of Things Collections College Archives Research Guides Student Support Services Faculty Resources Heinz Franzen Learning Center Registrar Registrar Dean’s List Transcripts Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Areas of Study Learn more about our areas of study to decide if Ripon is right for you! Areas of Study Admissions Overview Overview Connect With Us Virtual Map Visit Visit On-Campus Visits Campus Events Fall Preview Day Private Group Visits Apply Apply to Ripon First-Year Applicants Graduate Applicants International Applicants Transfer Applicants Home-schooled Applicants Financial Aid Cost and Financial Aid Affordability The Ripon Commitment Net Price Calculator Apply For Financial Aid Billing Scholarships and Types of Aid Military Benefits Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Your First Year Financial Aid Offer Accepted Students Accepted Students Deposit Now Student Status Register for Orientation Accepted Student Events Welcome Week Student Life Overview Overview Living in Ripon Housing & Dining Living Options Ripon College Dining Clubs & Activities Clubs and Organizations Greek Life ROTC Center for Diversity and Inclusion Esports Campus Services Health and Wellness Parking and Transportation Campus Safety Resources MyRipon Portal Harassment/Title IX Bias Related Incident Protocol Hazing News & Events News News Media Contact Events Events Calendar Plan your event Fine Art Events Arts Events Music Events Theatre Events Ceremonies & Special Events Welcome Week Catalyst Day Commencement Alumni Weekend Family Weekend and Homecoming Publications Publications Athletics Master’s Programs Apply Graduate Applicants Graduate Tuition Fees and Aid Request Information Create a Career with Endurance Gain the high-demand skills and the freedom to make your mark through Ripon College’s new Master of Exercise Science program. From the new connections to the unexpected answers, all that you discover here will prepare you for an extraordinary career. Master of Exercise Science View Area of Study Graduate Admissions Ready to turn your passion for wellness into an extraordinary career? See below how to pursue your Master of Exercise Science at Ripon College and create a career with endurance. Requirements Résumé including experiences relevant to exercise science, research and other related activities. 3.00 college GPA* Letter of interest Official transcripts from all post-secondary institutions attended Three letters of recommendation No GRE requirement No requirement for specific undergraduate majors/minors A maximum of 9 hours of graduate coursework completed before a student is admitted to the graduate program may count toward meeting the requirements of the master’s degree Prerequisites of one semester of anatomy and physiology and exercise physiology are required for the exercise physiology specialization only No prerequisites are required for sport management or sport psychology Students admitted to the graduate program lacking the recommended undergraduate coursework may be required to complete courses in these areas to meet prerequisites for specific graduate courses. International applicants are not eligible for admission to the program based on requirements from the US Department of State. *Provisional admission status may be granted with a 2.75-2.99 GPA. Provisional admission students will earn full status after completing 9 graduate credits with a minimum 3.00 GPA. 4+1 direct entry For Undergraduates pursuing the master’s direct entry program Get a head start on your graduate degree while completing your undergraduate education at Ripon College. Through Ripon’s 4+1 Direct Entry program, eligible students can secure their place in the master’s program as early as their first year at Ripon and continue seamlessly into graduate study after completing their bachelor’s degree. Students are considered for the 4+1 Direct Entry program after being admitted to Ripon College and submitting a Letter of Interest. Applications are reviewed on a rolling basis, so students can express their interest and receive an admission decision throughout the year. Once accepted into the direct-entry program, you’ll be accelerating your career and graduating with a master’s degree within 12 to 15 months after you complete your undergraduate education. To be considered for the 4+1 Direct Entry: Be admitted to Ripon College as an undergraduate student. Submit the 4+1 Direct Entry Letter of Interest. Applications are reviewed and admissions decisions are made on a rolling basis. Once enrolled at Ripon, maintain a minimum 3.0 GPA and complete required prerequisite courses to remain eligible for the graduate program. Apply Request Information Undergraduate 4+1 requirements Mark your interest in the 4+1 Direct-Entry program on your undergraduate application Earn a 3.00 high school unweighted GPA or above on a 4.00 scale Complete a biology and chemistry course in high school Essay Requirements Submit a letter describing how your interest in exercise science has evolved: What are some of your long-term career goals? In what ways do you hope the 4+1 program will help achieve these goals? What experiences have you had or skills/traits do you possess that will make you successful in the program and field of exercise science? Your letter must be one-page in length in 12-point, Times New Roman font. At the top of your letter, please include the following information: Your full name Your high school name Your email address Your mobile phone number This information will help us contact you quickly if we have any clarifying questions we need to ask during the review process. Please submit the letter of interest as a Word Doc, Google Doc or PDF. We will not accept screenshots from note apps or other messaging applications. Letters may be submitted to adminfo@ripon.edu . Living Arrangements Graduate students have the option to choose housing in the on-campus apartments at Ripon College. Housing is available on a first-come, first-serve basis. You also can choose to have a meal plan if desired.* You are not required to live on campus or have a meal plan as a graduate student. *Meal plans are not available during summer terms. Campus Apartments I chose the Master of Exercise Science at Ripon College because the small class sizes create an environment where I can grow both academically and personally. Ripon's commitment to hands-on learning and individualized support stood out to me right away. Whether its working closely with faculty on research, getting involved in campus organizations that align with my interest, or taking advantage of internship opportunities, I've always felt encouraged to push myself and explore the areas I'm passionate about. Kelly Kline Exercise Physiology Affordability and Tuition Ready to elevate your career?  Learn more about graduate program tuition and fees at Ripon, and how to apply for aid. Graduate Tuition Application Timeline The start term for the Master of Exercise Science program is Summer 2027. Graduate application are open now. All application materials must be submitted by April 1, 2027. Apply Now DO MORE YOUR WAY If you are interested in learning more about Ripon College and the Master of Exercise Science program, we invite you to schedule a virtual appointment or on-campus visit.  Complete the appointment request form to be contacted by a Graduate Admission Coordinator today! Request Information Apply Today Contact Brittany Followay Quick Links MyRipon Portal Red Hawks Athletics Lane Library Make a Gift Admissions Request Information Visit Apply Resources Campus Map Course Catalog Connect With Us Jobs at Ripon Consumer Information Ripon College 300 W. Seward St. Ripon, WI 54971 P: 920-748-8115 E: adminfo@ripon.edu Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy . Questions about Ripon College? Get answers in 20 seconds!"
    },
    "https://ripon.edu/catalyst-curriculum/": {
      "title": "Catalyst Curriculum",
      "description": "Catalyst Curriculum |  Ripon College Skip to Main Content About About Ripon Why Ripon Ripon at a Glance Mission and Vision Strategic Plan Leadership Community Resources Ripon College History Ripon, WI Fact Sheet Support Ripon Support Giving Ways to Give Where to Give Impact of Giving Engage Engage Alumni Events Alumni Weekend R Connections Update Information Parents and Families Volunteer Opportunities Oral Histories Project (PCI) Community Resources Community Resources Willmore Center Ceresco Prairie Conservancy Art Galleries Give to Ripon Show your support for the college and its students with a donation. Give to Ripon Academics Overview Overview Areas of Study Master’s Programs Centers and Resources STEM Faculty Directory Endowed Chairs and Professors Course Catalog Academic Calendar Experiential Learning Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry Lane Library & Archives Lane Library Library Information Library of Things Collections College Archives Research Guides Student Support Services Faculty Resources Heinz Franzen Learning Center Registrar Registrar Dean’s List Transcripts Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Areas of Study Learn more about our areas of study to decide if Ripon is right for you! Areas of Study Admissions Overview Overview Connect With Us Virtual Map Visit Visit On-Campus Visits Campus Events Fall Preview Day Private Group Visits Apply Apply to Ripon First-Year Applicants Graduate Applicants International Applicants Transfer Applicants Home-schooled Applicants Financial Aid Cost and Financial Aid Affordability The Ripon Commitment Net Price Calculator Apply For Financial Aid Billing Scholarships and Types of Aid Military Benefits Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Your First Year Financial Aid Offer Accepted Students Accepted Students Deposit Now Student Status Register for Orientation Accepted Student Events Welcome Week Student Life Overview Overview Living in Ripon Housing & Dining Living Options Ripon College Dining Clubs & Activities Clubs and Organizations Greek Life ROTC Center for Diversity and Inclusion Esports Campus Services Health and Wellness Parking and Transportation Campus Safety Resources MyRipon Portal Harassment/Title IX Bias Related Incident Protocol Hazing News & Events News News Media Contact Events Events Calendar Plan your event Fine Art Events Arts Events Music Events Theatre Events Ceremonies & Special Events Welcome Week Catalyst Day Commencement Alumni Weekend Family Weekend and Homecoming Publications Publications Athletics Catalyst Curriculum Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry With Catalyst Curriculum Do More in Four With our five-course Catalyst curriculum, you can do it all in just four years. This one-of-a-kind academic flexibility allows you to complete multiple majors and minors, participate in research, play sports, hold an internship — you name it. All without having to take a long list of general education courses! Most importantly, you can build the communication and critical-thinking skills you need to excel in life and in the workplace. Every Ripon College graduate demonstrates they’ve mastered the skills of: Public speaking Writing Critical thinking Group collaboration Quantitative reasoning Information literacy Intercultural competence To learn more about individual Catalyst sections, please click here : each PDF file includes course descriptions, major assignments, and information about each class. View  Catalyst Sections Catalyst 110: Writing Writing - Information Literacy - Critical Thinking - Collaboration Catalyst 120: Quantitative Reasoning Oral Communication - Information Literacy - Critical Thinking Catalyst 210: Cultural Competence Critical Thinking - Writing Information - Literacy Intercultural Catalyst 220: Integration Oral Communication - Information Literacy - Collaboration - Writing Integration Catalyst 300: Collaborative Capstone Presentation - Public Speaking Catalyst Day Presentations Catalyst Day—held each fall and spring—is designed to showcase Ripon College’s Catalyst curriculum and celebrate the achievements of the students who are completing the junior-level collaborative capstone. Throughout the day, attendees will have the opportunity to watch teams of students present problem solving projects that tackle worldwide issues. Go to Catalyst Day Challenges Catalyst 300 students are tasked with developing solutions to prominent real-world issues. Catalyst Day Topics 2026 Conserving Wisconsin's Natural Resources Since 1986, the Natural Resources Foundation of Wisconsin has sought to protect Wisconsin’s natural heritage through conservation, private-sector engagement and outreach programs, and regularly funds projects that have a beneficial influence on the environment. It currently offers grant monies to organizations that protect state lands, waters, and wildlife, and that educate the public about environmental issues. (https://www.wisconservation.org/) Identify a significant problem related to a natural resource in Wisconsin, research it, and write a proposal to address it that you think the foundation might fund. To be successful, you must conduct intensive research on the problem, analyze its various components, and propose innovative solutions to improve them. Food Food is essential to human survival: it fuels our bodies and provides livelihoods. Our lands and oceans produce enough food – yet a third is wasted every year. Over 11% of people in the world are hungry and 3.1 million children die as a result of malnutrition annually. In the United States, one in eight people do not know where their next meal might come from (Feeding America, 2018). The future of our food supply will be further challenged by climate change, environmental degradation, and worldwide poverty and inequality. The goal of this case is to ensure continued support for agriculture and fishing so that current and future generations are able to produce and have access to safe, healthy, high quality, and culturally appropriate food. Sustainable Campus Universities and colleges are leaders in knowledge creation and practice. Sustainability offers a unique opportunity to higher education: universities can use their campuses and communities as “living laboratories” to research best practices and innovate to promote sustainability. The Association for Advancement of Sustainability in Higher Education (AASHE) establishes sustainability priorities and documents successes in academics, engagement, operations, inclusion, wellbeing, and finance (https://www.aashe.org/ ). This challenge therefore addresses sustainability on a college campus, more specifically at Ripon College, and focuses on promoting sustainability on campus and in the surrounding community. The Body Reimagined The human body is often described as the “most complex machine ever created,” yet it remains a site of profound biological limitations, social contention, and ethical dilemmas. Whether viewed through the lens of medicine, aesthetics, law, psychology, or culture, the body is not just a biological entity—it is a lived experience and a social construct. Identify a specific friction point where the human body intersects with modern society. Research this problem from at least two distinct disciplinary perspectives (e.g., Biology and Philosophy, or Exercise Science and Sociology…) and design an innovative proposal—a policy, a physical prototype, a digital tool, or a community program—to address it and improve the human experience. Please make sure your topic is appropriate for classroom and public presentation. The Loneliness Epidemic A report by the U.S. Surgeon General in 2023 signaled that Americans of various ages and social groups suffer from an actual loneliness epidemic, in particular teens and young adults, elderly people, minorities, and people with lower income levels. Community involvement is lower than before; jobs require less in-person social interaction; social media create contacts, but do not seem to cultivate the authentic connections that allow humans to really thrive. This loneliness can lead to anxiety, depression, and addiction, and lack of human contact can aggravate dementia in older adults. What can be done to rebuild a lasting sense of community and authentic forms of communication among people? Your challenge is to identify a specific problem related to the loneliness epidemic and to come up with an innovative plan to address it. As you develop your project, focus on community building and social programming rather than on individualized therapies. Design your project so that it actually can be put into practice. Theatre For Social Change An Enemy of the People, The Laramie Project, Sweat, and A Raisin in the Sun are four well-known examples of plays that have addressed social issues of their times. Drama has the ability to deeply engage audiences with and to create empathy for individuals involved in social quagmires in ways that policy papers or news articles cannot. After reading these example plays, your group will compose and perform a ten-minute play on CTL Day that addresses one or more of the following social issues. Your play will be accompanied by a research paper that analyzes your chosen issue and provides solutions to address it: Truth vs. conformity Gender inequality and sexuality Rising housing costs and housing discrimination Public health Rural or urban poverty See Research Guide Because of the Catalyst Curriculum and its replacement of general courses, I was able to graduate early with two minors, join a sorority and participate in several student organizations. Olivia Robinson ’23 History, Minors in Museum Studies, Anthropology DO MORE YOUR WAY Become the extraordinary individual you are meant to be at Ripon. Areas of Study Experiential Learning Centers and Resources Quick Links MyRipon Portal Red Hawks Athletics Lane Library Make a Gift Admissions Request Information Visit Apply Resources Campus Map Course Catalog Connect With Us Jobs at Ripon Consumer Information Ripon College 300 W. Seward St. Ripon, WI 54971 P: 920-748-8115 E: adminfo@ripon.edu Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy . Questions about Ripon College? Get answers in 20 seconds!"
    },
    "https://ripon.edu/center-for-politics-and-the-people/": {
      "title": "Center for Politics and the People",
      "description": "Center for Politics and the People |  Ripon College Skip to Main Content About About Ripon Why Ripon Ripon at a Glance Mission and Vision Strategic Plan Leadership Community Resources Ripon College History Ripon, WI Fact Sheet Support Ripon Support Giving Ways to Give Where to Give Impact of Giving Engage Engage Alumni Events Alumni Weekend R Connections Update Information Parents and Families Volunteer Opportunities Oral Histories Project (PCI) Community Resources Community Resources Willmore Center Ceresco Prairie Conservancy Art Galleries Give to Ripon Show your support for the college and its students with a donation. Give to Ripon Academics Overview Overview Areas of Study Master’s Programs Centers and Resources STEM Faculty Directory Endowed Chairs and Professors Course Catalog Academic Calendar Experiential Learning Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry Lane Library & Archives Lane Library Library Information Library of Things Collections College Archives Research Guides Student Support Services Faculty Resources Heinz Franzen Learning Center Registrar Registrar Dean’s List Transcripts Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Areas of Study Learn more about our areas of study to decide if Ripon is right for you! Areas of Study Admissions Overview Overview Connect With Us Virtual Map Visit Visit On-Campus Visits Campus Events Fall Preview Day Private Group Visits Apply Apply to Ripon First-Year Applicants Graduate Applicants International Applicants Transfer Applicants Home-schooled Applicants Financial Aid Cost and Financial Aid Affordability The Ripon Commitment Net Price Calculator Apply For Financial Aid Billing Scholarships and Types of Aid Military Benefits Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Your First Year Financial Aid Offer Accepted Students Accepted Students Deposit Now Student Status Register for Orientation Accepted Student Events Welcome Week Student Life Overview Overview Living in Ripon Housing & Dining Living Options Ripon College Dining Clubs & Activities Clubs and Organizations Greek Life ROTC Center for Diversity and Inclusion Esports Campus Services Health and Wellness Parking and Transportation Campus Safety Resources MyRipon Portal Harassment/Title IX Bias Related Incident Protocol Hazing News & Events News News Media Contact Events Events Calendar Plan your event Fine Art Events Arts Events Music Events Theatre Events Ceremonies & Special Events Welcome Week Catalyst Day Commencement Alumni Weekend Family Weekend and Homecoming Publications Publications Athletics Center for Politics and the People Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry THE SPACE TO ENGAGE The Center for Politics and the People was established to promote constructive political debate and inform citizens concerned with good governance. Together, students and faculty plan year-round events that reflect the rich diversity of political thought. By fostering civic engagement and encouraging constructive debate, we create a space for students and the larger community to engage with public affairs. OUR PLACE IN POLITICS Ripon, Wisconsin, is the perfect stage for political engagement. The Republican Party was born here, as was women’s suffrage leader Carrie Chapman Catt. The National Forensics League was founded at Ripon College. Our alumni go on to hold positions in government, shape politics and form public policy at all levels. If you are passionate about public affairs, Ripon is a storied place to be. Mission Statement The Center for Politics and the People at Ripon College was created to promote constructive political debate, analyze public policy and foster the engagement of citizens and their government. Inspired by the principles of the American founders and the legacies of our great leaders, The Center will create a forum for dialogue, research, analysis and dissemination of ideas about local, state, national and international issues. Policymakers, academics, experts, candidates and politicians will present and defend their ideas and solutions for the benefit of the country, people and community. Students will learn how to pursue careers in politics and government. The electorate will find scholarly, accessible and relevant research that connects political thought and effective governance. My interactions with those at events sponsored by the Center have shown me that politics is something that everyone, no matter education or background, can participate in. Preston Reese-Grimm Class of 2026 Every event exposed me to different perspectives on a variety of issues in the political sphere. I have grown as a political science student, a leader, and a person. Angelica Martorello Class of 2026 I started attending the Center of People and Politics events as a first-year student and now am on the Student Advisory Board. It gives me a place to learn more about issues in politics with those around me who are also interested. Alivia Roeglin Class of 2025 Internship Fellowship The Center for Politics and People offers $2,000 Internship Fellowships to Ripon students with internships in public affairs. The award is intended to help cover costs like missed income, gas money and rental assistance while completing the internship. It is not meant to cover all expenses. Five fellowships are typically granted each year on a rolling basis depending on available funding. Criteria We invite Ripon students in good standing to apply. To qualify, your internship must align with the mission of the Center. Academic achievement, along with how the internship will contribute to your career goals (see Process), will also be considered. While your internship does not need to be secured at the time of application, stipend funds will only be disbursed once a signed agreement with your internship site is provided. This agreement must be shared with the Center directors immediately. Process With your application, you’ll need to provide a brief essay explaining: A description of the internship How your internship will help you pursue a career in public affairs Any other pertinent information you’d like to share Contact the Director of the Center for an application. The application and essay must be submitted to the Director of the Center. Once received, a decision will be made within two weeks. CONTACT AND ADVISORY BOARDS Center Director Henrik Schatzinger Professor of Political Science, Chair of Political Science Department Email 2025-26 Board Members Our student board members provide input on the Center’s programming and events. Noah Hinkfuss ’26 Angelica Martorello ’26 Karly Riverdahl ‘26 Preston Benton ‘29 External Advisory Board Members Judge Jon Wilcox ’58 Bob Welch ’92 Rick Lewandowski ’75 Robb Kahl ’94 Bill MacLeod ’73 Politics and the people  EVENTS Over the years, the Center has hosted events with elected officials, policymakers, academic experts, high-level campaign operatives, and citizens from across the political spectrum. Many of these events are open to the community. Watch Previous Events The CPP and Wisconsin Eye have teamed up to record and hold these recordings from the Fall 2025 season. You will need to create an account to view the videos. Global Trade, Local Stakes The Misinformation Machine Previous Events Spring 2025 Apr. 3, 2025: The Global US and the War on Gaza: America, Israel, and the Politics of Religious Dissent Watch a recording of the event here . Feb. 25, 2025: The Conservative Environmentalist Watch a recording of the event here . Fall 2024 Nov. 12, 2024: After the Election: Now What? Watch a recording of the event here . Oct. 25, 2024: Panel Discussion on the 2024 Election Watch a recording of the event here . Sept. 26, 2024: Asylum and the Changing Face of Immigration Watch a recording of the event here . Sept. 5, 2024: From Pen to Perception: The Power of Political Cartoons Watch a recording of the event here . DO MORE YOUR WAY Become the extraordinary individual you are meant to be at Ripon. Areas of Study Experiential Learning Centers and Resources Quick Links MyRipon Portal Red Hawks Athletics Lane Library Make a Gift Admissions Request Information Visit Apply Resources Campus Map Course Catalog Connect With Us Jobs at Ripon Consumer Information Ripon College 300 W. Seward St. Ripon, WI 54971 P: 920-748-8115 E: adminfo@ripon.edu Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy . Questions about Ripon College? Get answers in 20 seconds!"
    },
